the basic principles of this refactor:

1. gui has something called a "datamodel" that lets you spawn multiple copies of a 
gui element based on a list. vanilla uses this a lot with hardcoded lists: for 
example, using the datamodel GetPlayer.GetKnights and then spawning a button for 
every knight of the player, and each of those buttons would have access to the 
knight's data and can display dynamic content like the knight's name, portrait, etc
2. you can use arbitrary variable lists from script as datamodels, with 
Character.MakeScope.GetList('listname') (for variable lists) and 
GetGlobalList('listname') (for global lists)
3. you can use story_cycles as "custom scopes" since they're lightweight and can 
store variables

if you use variable lists of story_cycles, you can make GUI do anything you want.

so here's my proposal. at the start of the game, create a story_cycle for every 
duel move and put it in a global variable list. then make the attributes for each 
duel move modular. like, if the duel move has an energy cost, add an energy_cost 
variable to the story_cycle. but if it doesn't, you don't. same with all the other 
attributes a duel move can have.

this means you only need one effect and one trigger to evaluate any duel move in 
the game. to execute a duel move you read the story_cycle and check for the 
presence of the modules. if there's an energy_cost variable, deduct the energy. if 
there's a damage_value, apply damage. if there's a morale_damage_value, do morale 
damage. etc. if the variable doesn't exist on the story_cycle, you don't need to do 
anything.

lists are references so you only need to create the story_cycles once. for the 
loadout selection gui, use the global list of duel moves as a datamodel, and when 
you select a move for your loadout, save it in a variable list on the player. in 
the duel screen, you then use the player's variable list as a datamodel.

all that means you can get rid of 90% of the boilerplate. the AI move selection can 
do random_in_list on the list of duel moves. when checking the size of your 
loadout, you can just check the size of your variable list instead of manually 
checking hundreds of variables.

because all the data of the duel moves is readily available, you can read it 
directly from the variables on the story_cycle and create the duel move 
descriptions dynamically (e.g. read the AP cost from a variable instead of 
"hardcoding" it in localization). that also means you can create custom filters and 
sort moves by AP cost or damage or whatever. or let the player toggle between 
different UI modes (compact/detailed/spreadsheet views)



there is only one missing link, which is, how do you limit what duel moves a 
character can select in the first place. by which i mean splat limitations or perk 
requirements (not immediate requirements like AP/energy costs, you can read those 
from the story_cycle as mentioned). there is a janky way to do this, though.

decisions are scopes. decisions can be saved as variables, and you can check their 
triggers with can_execute_decision. this allows you to save "triggers" as 
variables, which is sort of like a Callable or lambda in other programming 
languages. sort of. the downside is the decisions have to be visible to make this 
work, so this also needs a vanilla edit to hide decisions from a specific category. 
but it's a small and non-invasive edit so it's fine.

so then, each story_cycle has a decision saved on it, and each decision has the 
duel move's requirements in the is_valid_showing_failures_only block, like 
has_perk = xyz or whatever. you can then check these in the loadout editor or the 
AI evaluation function.

nice side effect is that the decision can pull double duty and we can use the 
decision's name and tooltip for the duel move's loc, and the decision illustration 
to make the buttons look better.



to summarize, you then only need to edit 3 files to add or change a duel move:

1. the effect that initializes the global list of duel moves at the start of the 
game, where all the modular effects are set (cost, damage, and other unique effects)
2. the decision file with the trigger to unlock the duel move, and the illustration
3. the loc file with the duel move's name and tooltip

this can easily be done by hand.